Z={
1:"Commentators note that Arjuna uses three names for Kṛṣṇa in one verse: mahā-bāho, Hṛṣīkeśa and Keśi-niṣūdana. Madhusūdana says this shows his eagerness at the close of the teaching. Jñāneśvar devotes nearly a fifth of his whole commentary to this final chapter, calling it the temple’s golden pinnacle.",
2:"The order of sannyāsins founded in Śaṅkara’s tradition, the Daśanāmī order, still gives monks the ochre robe and new names. Vaiṣṇava and other traditions have their own renunciant orders. The Gītā honours formal renunciation but teaches that tyāga, inner letting go, is open to everyone, monk or householder.",
3:"Commentators note manīṣiṇaḥ, “thinkers”, for those who say action is a fault. Madhusūdana says they reason that every action involves some harm, even breathing kills tiny creatures. The Jain tradition takes this concern very seriously, with monks sweeping the path to avoid harming insects. The Gītā accepts the concern but not the conclusion.",
4:"Commentators note that relinquishment is called “of three kinds”, trividha, echoing the threefold classifications of chapter 17. Śaṅkara says the purpose is to show that only sāttvika relinquishment leads to freedom. The structure of the chapter now follows the guṇas closely until 18.40.",
5:"Commentators note pāvanāni, “purifiers”: sacrifice, charity and austerity cleanse the mind. Madhusūdana says even the wise continue them so as not to mislead others (3.21–26). Many great teachers kept simple daily practices to the end of their lives: prayer, study, charity.",
6:"Commentators note etāny api tu karmāṇi, “but even these actions”: the highest religious acts too must be done without attachment. Śaṅkara says this is the key that turns action into a means of freedom. Rāmānuja says it makes all action worship.",
7:"Commentators note niyata karma, obligatory action: duties one is bound to by one’s role. Madhusūdana says abandoning them out of delusion is the worst kind of giving up. Parents who neglect children in the name of spirituality, or officials who abandon their posts, are examples many teachers give.",
8:"Commentators note that rājasika relinquishment “does not gain its fruit”, the peace that true renunciation brings. Śaṅkara says it is like a merchant who leaves his shop to avoid hard work: he loses both profit and rest. Fear of discomfort is not a spiritual reason.",
9:"Commentators note saṅgaṃ tyaktvā phalaṃ caiva, “giving up both attachment and fruit”: two things are dropped. Madhusūdana says attachment is to the act itself, fruit is the result. The sāttvika relinquisher holds neither. Vinoba Bhave wrote that this verse alone could guide a whole life.",
10:"Commentators note medhāvī, “wise”, and chinna-saṃśaya, “doubts cut”: the relinquisher is clear-minded. Śaṅkara says such a person does not avoid unpleasant duties or cling to pleasant ones. Many workers know the test: doing the boring or difficult parts of a job with the same care as the enjoyable parts.",
11:"Commentators note aśeṣataḥ, “entirely”: no embodied being can give up all action. Śaṅkara says even the knower breathes, eats and moves. Rāmānuja says this is why the Gītā teaches tyāga rather than outer renunciation. The verse brings the debate of chapters 3 and 5 to a close.",
12:"Commentators note pretya, “after death”: the fruits of action are reaped in future lives. Śaṅkara says the renunciant who knows the Self reaps none. Rāmānuja says the one who offers all to the Lord is free of these fruits. The idea of karma as seeds that ripen later is central to Indian thought.",
13:"Commentators note kṛtānte, “in the conclusion” or “in the final teaching”. Madhusūdana says knowing the five causes, one sees that the Self is not the doer. A modern parallel is the way accident investigators look for many contributing causes rather than one person to blame.",
14:"Commentators note pṛthak-ceṣṭāḥ, “various efforts”: every action involves many separate efforts of breath, muscle and mind. Madhusūdana says daiva is the unseen power that makes these efforts succeed or fail. A farmer can plough, sow and water, but rain is beyond him.",
15:"Commentators note nyāyyaṃ vā viparītaṃ vā, “right or wrong”: the same five causes are at work in good and bad actions. What differs is the direction given by understanding. Śaṅkara says this shows the Self is never the doer of either.",
16:"Commentators note kevalam ātmānam, “the Self alone”: the mistake is thinking the pure Self acts by itself. Madhusūdana says such a person does not see, though he looks. The Gītā has made this point many times (3.27, 5.8, 13.29); here it closes the analysis of action.",
17:"Commentators note buddhir yasya na lipyate, “whose understanding is not tainted”: freedom depends on a pure mind. Madhusūdana says this state is rare and not to be claimed lightly. Arjuna is not told he has reached it; he is shown the ideal he can move toward.",
18:"Commentators note karma-codanā, “the impulse to action”, and karma-saṅgraha, “the basis of action”. Śaṅkara explains the first three as what moves us to act, the second three as what makes up the act. The analysis is similar to modern ideas of motivation and behaviour.",
19:"Commentators note yathāvat, “as they are”: Kṛṣṇa promises an accurate account. Madhusūdana says the purpose is to help the seeker recognise the sāttvika and cultivate it. The analyses of 18.20–40 are often taught in schools of management and psychology in India today.",
20:"Commentators note ekaṃ bhāvam avyayam, “one imperishable reality”. Jñāneśvar compares sāttvika knowledge to seeing gold in many ornaments: bangles, chains and rings differ, but the gold is one. The vision of 13.27 is here called sāttvika knowledge.",
21:"Commentators note pṛthaktvena, “as separate”: rājasika knowledge sees each being as entirely separate. Madhusūdana says it leads to likes and dislikes, mine and theirs. Much conflict, from family quarrels to wars, grows from this kind of seeing.",
22:"Commentators note alpa, “small” or “narrow”: tāmasika knowledge is limited and unreasoned. Śaṅkara gives the example of thinking one’s body is the whole self. Jñāneśvar compares it to a frog in a well who thinks the well is the ocean.",
23:"Commentators note arāga-dveṣataḥ, “without love or hatred”: sāttvika action is impartial. Madhusūdana says such action is done because it ought to be done. Many professional codes, for doctors, judges and teachers, rest on this ideal.",
24:"Commentators note sāhaṅkāreṇa, “with ego”: rājasika action is done with a strong sense of “I”. Śaṅkara says it is done for desired results. Many find that work done from ego is more tiring than work done from duty or love.",
25:"Commentators note anapekṣya, “without regard”: tāmasika action ignores consequences. Madhusūdana says it is begun in delusion. The Mahābhārata’s dice game, played despite every warning, is a classic example of tāmasika action and its consequences.",
26:"Commentators note anahaṃ-vādī, “one who does not boast”: the sāttvika doer does not talk about himself. Śaṅkara says he is unchanged in success and failure. Many great craftsmen in India worked anonymously; the builders of most temples are unknown.",
27:"Commentators note lubdha, “greedy”, and aśuci, “impure”: the rājasika doer is driven by want. Madhusūdana says he swings between joy and grief with every result. The verse is a mirror for anyone in a high-pressure job.",
28:"Commentators note viṣādī, “despondent”: the tāmasika doer gives up easily. Śaṅkara explains śaṭha as deceitful. Jñāneśvar describes such a person as one who sleeps through the day and complains at night that nothing has been done.",
29:"Commentators note pṛthaktvena, “separately”: understanding and resolve are treated as two faculties. Madhusūdana says understanding decides and resolve holds. Both are needed: a clear map and the strength to walk it.",
30:"Commentators note bandhaṃ mokṣaṃ ca, “bondage and freedom”: sāttvika understanding knows what binds and what frees. Śaṅkara says it knows the path of action and the path of renunciation. This is the understanding Arjuna lacked in chapter 1.",
31:"Commentators note ayathāvat, “not as it is”: rājasika understanding sees dharma partially. Madhusūdana says it is clouded by desire. Many ethical mistakes are of this kind: half-right, bent by self-interest.",
32:"Commentators note sarvārthān viparītān, “all things reversed”: tāmasika understanding sees everything upside down. Śaṅkara says it takes adharma for dharma. Jñāneśvar compares it to an owl, which sees in the dark and is blind in daylight.",
33:"Commentators note avyabhicāriṇyā, “unwavering”: sāttvika resolve does not shift with moods. Madhusūdana says it holds the mind, breath and senses on the path. Steady daily practice, even for a few minutes, builds this resolve.",
34:"Commentators note prasaṅgena, “with attachment”: rājasika resolve pursues dharma, wealth and pleasure for their fruits. Śaṅkara says it is the resolve of one who wants results. The three aims are honoured in Hindu tradition, but held this way they keep one bound.",
35:"Commentators note durmedhā, “foolish”: tāmasika resolve holds on to sleep, fear and grief. Madhusūdana says it is steadiness in the wrong things. Teachers note that the cure is not to stop being determined, but to redirect determination toward good ends.",
36:"Commentators note duḥkhāntaṃ ca nigacchati, “reaches the end of sorrow”: sāttvika happiness leads to freedom from sorrow. Śaṅkara says it grows with practice. The verse begins the Gītā’s last great analysis, of happiness, which everyone seeks.",
37:"Commentators note ātma-buddhi-prasāda-jam, “born of the clarity of one’s own understanding”: sāttvika happiness comes from within. Madhusūdana says it begins bitter because discipline is hard. Teachers compare it to medicine, bitter to taste but healing.",
38:"Commentators note viṣaya-indriya-saṃyogāt, “from contact of senses and objects”: rājasika happiness comes from outside. Śaṅkara says it ends in loss of strength, beauty, wisdom and wealth. Jñāneśvar compares it to honey on the edge of a sword.",
39:"Commentators note mohanam ātmanaḥ, “deluding the self”: tāmasika happiness clouds the mind from start to finish. Śaṅkara says it arises from sleep, sloth and carelessness. Many teachers warn against confusing numbness with peace.",
40:"Commentators note pṛthivyāṃ vā divi vā, “on earth or in heaven”: even the gods are made of the guṇas. Madhusūdana says only the Self beyond them is free. The verse humbles all beings and points beyond nature.",
}
for _k,_t in Z.items():
    _a=DEEP.get(_k,('','',''))
    DEEP[_k]=(_a[0],(_a[1]+' '+_t).strip(),_a[2])
